# Binned position scales: scale_binned

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_x_binned()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_binned.html), which covers
`scale_y_binned()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_x_binned()` and `scale_y_binned()` cut a continuous position into bins, so that a geom
needing discrete positions can use it: with `geom_bar()`, a binned x scale draws a histogram.
Giggly has neither yet: it has no scale functions, and a position scale is continuous or discrete
as it is chosen from the data.

## A histogram from bars

```r
# Create a histogram by binning the x-axis
ggplot(mtcars) +
  geom_bar(aes(mpg)) +
  scale_x_binned()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_x_binned()`.
